WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.633 --> 00:00:02.068
[dramatic music]

00:00:02.135 --> 00:00:05.305
NARRATOR: Along a roughly
25-mile stretch of shore,

00:00:05.372 --> 00:00:07.040
there have been
more than 60 shark

00:00:07.107 --> 00:00:11.878
attacks since records began
and almost half of them fatal.

00:00:15.715 --> 00:00:20.653
In fact, this small area
accounts for almost 50%

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:25.024
of recorded attacks in
the entire continent.

00:00:25.091 --> 00:00:29.729
As a result, it's earned a
chilling designation as South

00:00:29.796 --> 00:00:32.031
America's deadliest coastline.

00:00:32.098 --> 00:00:33.867
[water splashes]

00:00:37.137 --> 00:00:38.204
ANDRE AFONSO: In
Recife, the shark

00:00:38.271 --> 00:00:41.441
attack problem is very serious.

00:00:41.508 --> 00:00:43.943
It's probably one of the
most dangerous cities

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:46.780
in the world for having a swim.

00:00:46.846 --> 00:00:49.582
NARRATOR: Residents
are at their wits end.

00:00:49.649 --> 00:00:55.088
I want to know why it
happens here and always here.

00:00:55.155 --> 00:00:58.291
NARRATOR: In an attempt to
prevent further attacks,

00:00:58.358 --> 00:01:02.095
authorities banned
surfing in certain areas,

00:01:02.162 --> 00:01:05.932
and signs line the beaches
warning of the dangers

00:01:05.999 --> 00:01:08.334
of entering the ocean.

00:01:08.401 --> 00:01:12.539
But the only real way to
stop this bloody barrage

00:01:12.605 --> 00:01:15.275
is to figure out how it started.

00:01:15.341 --> 00:01:16.576
FABIO HAZIN: It's
absolutely crucial

00:01:16.643 --> 00:01:21.347
to find the cause of the
problem because, if we don't, we

00:01:21.414 --> 00:01:24.818
can't build a solution.

00:01:24.884 --> 00:01:27.053
NARRATOR: So what's driving
this terrifying onslaught

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:30.056
along with Recife's shores?

00:01:30.123 --> 00:01:34.427
And could other coastal
resorts be next?

00:01:39.399 --> 00:01:43.069
According to former
instructor Fernando Clark,

00:01:43.136 --> 00:01:47.540
something makes diving
in this area unique.

00:01:47.607 --> 00:01:50.810
And it hides deep
beneath the waves.

00:01:50.877 --> 00:01:55.114
Recife is the shipwrecked
capital off Brazil.

00:01:55.181 --> 00:01:57.484
NARRATOR: The area
around the attack zone

00:01:57.550 --> 00:02:01.354
is home to dozens of
sunken vessels, some

00:02:01.421 --> 00:02:05.024
dating as far back
as the 16th century.

00:02:05.091 --> 00:02:08.027
But according to Clark,
these ill-fated ships are

00:02:08.094 --> 00:02:11.498
more than just rusty relics.

00:02:11.564 --> 00:02:13.066
FERNANDO CLARK: The
shipwrecks become a reef

00:02:13.132 --> 00:02:16.703
completely full of marine life.

00:02:16.769 --> 00:02:21.407
NARRATOR: Fish,
crustaceans, and mollusks

00:02:21.474 --> 00:02:24.611
call these down vessels home.

00:02:24.677 --> 00:02:28.181
But it's what these marine
inhabitants attract that could

00:02:28.248 --> 00:02:30.984
be problematic for people.

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:32.719
FERNANDO CLARK: When you
go shipwreck diving here,

00:02:32.785 --> 00:02:37.123
most of the time we
see a lot of sharks.

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:39.926
NARRATOR: The presence
of sharks near shipwrecks

00:02:39.993 --> 00:02:43.796
is a phenomenon that can
be seen elsewhere too.

00:02:43.863 --> 00:02:46.833
In the US, sunken
vessels along the shores

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:51.771
of Oregon, Florida,
and North Carolina

00:02:51.838 --> 00:02:54.240
are inhabited by
these predators.

00:02:54.307 --> 00:02:55.708
The sharks are
attracted to the shipwreck

00:02:55.775 --> 00:02:58.778
because of the food.

00:02:58.845 --> 00:03:00.980
NARRATOR: So could
the wrecks of Recife

00:03:01.047 --> 00:03:03.583
be responsible for luring
dangerous predators

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:07.820
near shore where people are?

00:03:07.887 --> 00:03:11.524
Clark says the answer comes
down to the species of shark

00:03:11.591 --> 00:03:14.661
he sees most often on his dives.

00:03:14.727 --> 00:03:18.932
To demonstrate, he plunges
into the shark-infested waters.

00:03:18.998 --> 00:03:19.866
[water splashes]

00:03:19.933 --> 00:03:22.235
[gentle music]

00:03:26.606 --> 00:03:31.711
Before long, he spots just
what he's looking for--

00:03:31.778 --> 00:03:32.845
nurse sharks.

00:03:32.912 --> 00:03:34.881
[energetic music]

00:03:34.948 --> 00:03:38.017
Nurse sharks are
bottom dwellers,

00:03:38.084 --> 00:03:39.919
attracted to the
crustaceans inhabiting

00:03:39.986 --> 00:03:42.555
Recife's sunken ships.

00:03:42.622 --> 00:03:46.893
Measuring up to 9 feet,
they possess powerful jaws

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:49.562
with serrated teeth.

00:03:49.629 --> 00:03:52.498
And, worldwide, they
have been implicated

00:03:52.565 --> 00:03:56.869
in multiple attacks on humans.

00:03:56.936 --> 00:04:00.206
So could nurse sharks be
responsible for Recife's spike?

00:04:05.945 --> 00:04:09.515
Dr. Fabio Hazin is one
of the lead investigators

00:04:09.582 --> 00:04:11.551
trying to get to
the bottom of what's

00:04:11.618 --> 00:04:14.120
happening along this coast.

00:04:14.187 --> 00:04:17.724
And when he analyzes the
details of each incident,

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:19.993
he comes to an
important conclusion.

00:04:20.059 --> 00:04:23.563
FABIO HAZIN: Nobody in Recife
has ever reported being

00:04:23.630 --> 00:04:26.466
attacked by the nurse shark.

00:04:26.532 --> 00:04:30.870
So the shipwrecks are not at
all to blame for the attacks.

00:04:30.937 --> 00:04:33.973
NARRATOR: Instead,
Hazin's analysis points

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:37.543
to two other suspects,
both of which

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:40.647
have reputations for
attacking humans.

00:04:40.713 --> 00:04:44.484
The first one is tiger sharks.

00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:48.721
NARRATOR: Tiger sharks can
grow up to 18 feet in length

00:04:48.788 --> 00:04:51.924
and weigh in at 1,400 pounds.

00:04:51.991 --> 00:04:54.694
Known for their
undiscerning palates,

00:04:54.761 --> 00:04:57.797
they're equipped with
sharp, serrated teeth

00:04:57.864 --> 00:05:01.267
that allow them to
rip through seabirds,

00:05:01.334 --> 00:05:04.804
turtles, and even people.

00:05:04.871 --> 00:05:06.906
[dramatic music]

00:05:06.973 --> 00:05:10.176
Perhaps even more imposing
is the other culprit

00:05:10.243 --> 00:05:12.612
Hazin implicates in the spike.

00:05:12.679 --> 00:05:14.247
And that is bull sharks.

00:05:14.313 --> 00:05:16.916
[energetic music]

00:05:16.983 --> 00:05:19.185
NARRATOR: Bull sharks bite
with the most force pound

00:05:19.252 --> 00:05:22.422
for pound of any large shark.

00:05:22.488 --> 00:05:27.694
They are fast and aggressive.

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:31.431
And they're often found in the
same shallow coastal waters

00:05:31.497 --> 00:05:32.198
as humans.

00:05:35.501 --> 00:05:38.237
So it appears
tigers and bulls may

00:05:38.304 --> 00:05:40.907
be responsible for the spike.

00:05:40.973 --> 00:05:41.974
The question is, why?

